<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=cherokee-syllabary">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cherokee syllabary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 1820s</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tsalagi Digohwelodohdi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> syllabary is a writing system invented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sequoyah">Sequoyah</a> in the late 1810s and early 1820s to write the Cherokee language. Sequoyah, who was illiterate until its creation, began work around 1809, first tried a character for each word, then created characters for syllables, and worked on it for twelve years. Its 85 characters, originally 86, each represent a syllable. After its completion in the early 1820s it spread rapidly, and by 1825 the majority of Cherokees could read and write. In 1828 Elias Boudinot adapted the characters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> presses, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee-phoenix">Cherokee Phoenix</a> newspaper adopted it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cherokee-syllabary.jpg" alt="Cherokee syllabary" width="330" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lithographer: Lehman and Duval (George Lehman (d.1870); Peter S. Duval) Painter: Henry Inman (1801-20-28 - 1846-01-17); copy after a paintin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sequoyah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
